analysisWe need to translate the comment into an assert statement.

The method is set(int index, E element). It replaces element at index with new element, returns old value. The comment: 

"// @@@ If the index is non-negative and less than the initial size of the vector, then for every index greater than the specified index and less than the size of the vector, the element at that position remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was originally null."

We need to refer to initial size (before modification). Use \old(this.size()) for initial size. Also need to refer to element at positions > index and < size (presumably after modification). The condition: index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()). For every int i; i > index && i < this.size()? Wait size after set remains same (size unchanged). So we can use this.size() same as old size. But to be safe, we can use \old(this.size()) for bound. The element at position i after operation should equal the old element at i (or null if originally null). That's just equality: this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))? but if null, .equals would throw NPE. In examples they used .equals for objects. Use this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))? but if null, we need to handle null case. In earlier examples they used .equals for query(i). That assumes non-null? But they didn't handle null case. The comment says "remains unchanged or continues to be null if it was originally null." So equality (including null) can be expressed as (this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i)))? But that checks reference equality; for objects may be okay. Could use (\old(this.elementAt(i)) == null ? this.elementAt(i) == null : this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) but that's complex.

Given typical spec style, they may use .equals and assume non-null or that equals works with null? In Java, calling .equals on null throws NPE, but spec not executed. Usually they use .equals for objects; they may assume not null. However comment includes null case. Could use \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null ? this.elementAt(i) == null : this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))). But spec language may allow conditional expression.

Simplify: assert (index >=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; i>index && i< this.size(); this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)));

But need also handle null: maybe they accept .equals.

Thus final assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; i > index && i < this.size(); this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)));

Check parentheses: need parentheses around implication.

Thus final answer in code block.

